10:46:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Commercial supersonic passenger flights were not abolished by a single rule; Concorde was retired because it became economically and politically difficult to operate. Key reasons: - **Very high operating costs:** Concorde used enormous amounts of fuel, required specialised maintenance, and carried only around 100 passengers. Tickets had to be extremely expensive to make flights viable. - **Sonic-boom restrictions:** Flying faster than sound creates a loud sonic boom. Many countries banned or severely restricted supersonic flight over land, so Concorde was mainly limited to ocean routes such as London/Paris–New York. That greatly reduced the possible market. - **Noise and environmental concerns:** Its engines were very noisy around airports, and supersonic travel produces substantially higher fuel consumption and emissions per passenger than conventional long-haul aircraft. - **Small, ageing fleet:** Only 14 production Concordes entered airline service, split between British Airways and Air France. Supporting such a tiny and increasingly old fleet was expensive. - **Reduced demand and safety concerns:** The fatal Air France Concorde crash in 2000 damaged confidence and led to costly modifications. Although Concorde returned to service, demand—especially for premium travel—fell after the crash and after the 11 September 2001 attacks. - **No economic successor:** Other proposed supersonic airliners were cancelled because airlines and governments concluded they would be too costly, noisy, or environmentally problematic. So Concorde was not retired because supersonic flight was impossible—it was technically successful—but because it could not sustain a broad commercial business under the noise, cost, regulatory, and environmental constraints of the time. Supersonic flight still exists in military aviation and experimental aircraft. Several companies are again developing quieter, lower-emission supersonic passenger aircraft, though none is yet operating regular commercial service.
