10:46:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are not making it up—an article or documentary may well have said that—but it was likely using “banned” loosely or inaccurately. There was **no worldwide post-Concorde ban on supersonic passenger flights**. Concorde retired in 2003 because British Airways and Air France chose to end service; no global authority then outlawed the category of aircraft or passenger service. The important legal restriction is that many states prohibit or tightly control **supersonic civil flight over their territory**, because of sonic booms. For example, the United States has long generally prohibited civil aircraft from exceeding Mach 1 over US land without special authorisation. Similar practical restrictions exist in much of the world. That means a passenger SST would mainly be usable on routes over oceans. Concorde’s London/Paris–New York service was possible precisely because most of its supersonic segment was over the Atlantic. So the more accurate version is: > Supersonic passenger travel became effectively unavailable because it was heavily restricted over land and commercially unsustainable on the limited routes left—not because it was globally banned after Concorde retired. A documentary may have meant “banned on most routes” or “banned over inhabited land,” but “banned worldwide” is not technically correct.
